      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-136437",
        "knowledge_point_id": "3048320d-7346-53fd-819b-d6e32e4fc02c",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Distance Between a Particle and the Origin",
        "problem": "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(2i-4j)\\,\\text{m},$ with an acceleration of $(-i+j)\\,{\\text{m/min}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(3i-j)\\,\\text{m/min}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $6$ minutes.",
        "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=2i-4j,\\,u=3i-j,\\,a=-i+j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=6\\,\\text{min}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(2i-4j)+(3i-j)(6)+\\frac{1}{2}(-i+j)(6)^{2} \\\\  & =(2i-4j)+(18i-6j)+18(-i+j) \\\\  & =(20i-10j)+(-18i+18j) \\\\  & =(2i+8j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|2i+8j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
