        "knowledge_point_id": "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45",
        "knowledge_point": "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate",
        "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ estimate the value of $f(\\sqrt{2})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
        "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we have $f(0)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x-0) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(\\sqrt{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{2}) & ≈L(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an overestimate.",
