        "knowledge_point_id": "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary",
        "problem": "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+9t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+9t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,3{t}^{2}-12t+9⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),3({t}^{2}-4t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t+1)(t-1),3(t-1)(t-3)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t+1)(t-1),3(t-1)(t-3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)(t-1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 3(t-1)(t-3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
