      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-28219",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient",
        "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation.",
        "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2+\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2-\\text{i}))(x-(2+\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\text{i})-x(2-\\text{i})+(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-x\\text{i}-2x+x\\text{i}+4-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
