        "knowledge_point": "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form",
        "problem": "Express $z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$",
        "worked_solution": "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
